text:
Sam: quick ping — seeing duplicate side effects when our Go SDK retries after a 409/202 in some payment flows. also streaming clients sometimes fail to resume after a 5xx mid-stream. anyone seen weirdness or have recommended policies?
Maria: can you paste the minimal repro? usually duplicates mean we aren't setting an Idempotency-Key or the server treats retry as a new op. 409 sounds like a conflict, odd to retry that automatically. :thinking:
Evan: +1 on idempotency header. note a couple gotchas with Go: the default http.Client will resend on connection-level errors but if body was non-rewindable you might get partial requests. Also treat 429 specially — respect Retry-After header if present. 5xx generally retriable for idempotent ops.
Sam: repro attached in the issue but short version: POST /payments, network blip -> client retries, server returns 409 for a race then the client retries again and we create duplicate charge. streaming: websocket style stream got a 502 mid-chunk, client reconnects and fails to resume.
Lina: for streams, are you using a resume_token? if so we shouldn't re-POST the whole session, we should call resume endpoint with token+offset. if not, maybe implement checkpointing.
Evan: for retry policy I'd recommend:
```go
// pseudocode
if resp.StatusCode == 429 {
  // if Retry-After provided, sleep that long + small jitter
} else if isIdempotent(method) && (resp.StatusCode/100 == 5) {
  // exponential backoff with capped jitter
}
// for 409 or 422: do not auto-retry, bubble up to caller
```
Keep retries bounded (max 3 for non-idempotent, 4-6 for idempotent depending on latency budget).
Maria: small extra: if you're doing token refresh in middleware, watch out for refresh races. multiple goroutines refreshing at once can cause thundering herd and transient 401/429. Use singleflight or mutex around refresh and short backoff on refresh failures.
Sam: thanks — does anyone have a suggested backoff calc we can copy?
Evan: something we landed on: base=200ms, factor=2, max=30s, jitter = uniform(0, base*2^attempt) but cap at max. Example:
```go
func backoff(attempt int) time.Duration {
  base := 200 * time.Millisecond
  max := 30 * time.Second
  raw := base * (1 << attempt)
  jitter := time.Duration(rand.Int63n(int64(raw)))
  d := raw + jitter
  if d > max { return max }
  return d
}
```
Lina: for stream resume: protocol must return resume_token + consumed_offset on each ack. client should send `Resume-Token` header on reconnect. If server doesn't support that, add idempotency to operations inside stream so replays are safe.
DevEx Bot: docs: https://intra.redwood.ai/devex/retry-guidelines — see "sdk-resume" and "idempotency-keys" sections. Also file an issue so we can track cross-lang parity.
Sam: ok i'll update the Go SDK to: 1) require/propagate Idempotency-Key for mutating endpoints, 2) respect Retry-After for 429, 3) singleflight token refresh, 4) capped exp backoff with jitter, 5) stream resume via resume_token when available. PR + issue by EOD. :white_check_mark:
